  <h2 id="degree-myth">The degree-only myth vs the degree-optional truth</h2>

  <p>
    Neither extreme view survives contact with the actual data.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">"A degree is the only safe path"</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Doesn't explain why 11.2% of graduates are unemployed and only 42.6% are considered employable by industry standards.</li>
        <li>Ignores that 82% of employers say the real gap is practical skill, not credential count.</li>
        <li>Treats every degree as equal, when employability swings from roughly 58% to 78% depending on the field.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">"Degrees don't matter anymore, just build skills"</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Ignores that college dropouts, on average, earn less and face higher unemployment than graduates once you look past the billionaire outliers.</li>
        <li>Ignores that UPSC civil services, medicine, law practice, and chartered accountancy membership are legally degree-gated — no skill substitutes for the credential there.</li>
        <li>Underestimates how long real skill-building takes: 6-18 months of serious, structured work, not a weekend bootcamp.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Degrees are not irrelevant — they still open doors for regulated professions and specific top
    institutions where the credential itself carries weight. The real problem is degree-only thinking:
    treating the certificate as the whole plan, with no skill-building alongside it. A degree with a
    real skill portfolio, proof of work, and market positioning almost always beats either extreme alone.
  </p>

