  <h2 id="parents">How to have this conversation with your parents</h2>

  <p>
    In Indian households, a degree is often read as a stand-in for stability, family reputation, and
    proof that the years of school pressure led somewhere. Skipping it without a plan reads as risk. The
    fastest way to lower the temperature in this conversation is to arrive with more than a feeling.
  </p>

  <div class="rich-list-wrap">
    <ol class="rich-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Name the specific path, not "something in skills."</strong> "I want to do a full-stack
        bootcamp and become a developer" lands differently than "I want to try tech stuff."
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Bring the real cost and real timeline.</strong> Say the actual number and the actual
        6-18 month runway before income starts, not an optimistic guess.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Show one piece of current evidence.</strong> A live job posting for your target role, a
        placement report from the course, or a freelance gig already lined up changes the conversation
        from hope to plan.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Name your own backup plan out loud.</strong> What happens if this path does not work in
        12 months — a related degree still open, a different skill, or an exam track in parallel. Parents
        relax more when they see you have already thought about the downside.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Most family resistance is not really about the degree. It is about the absence of a visible plan.
      A no-degree path with a written timeline, a real cost estimate, and a stated backup usually gets
      taken far more seriously than a degree path chosen only because "everyone does it."
    </p>
  </div>

